<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viceroyalty of Peru</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1542">1542</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was the administrative unit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> created in 1542 to govern most of Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, made it a principal source of imperial revenue. Accounts of the Spanish Empire describe it, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, as one of the two primary administrative units of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru.jpg" alt="Viceroyalty of Peru" width="314" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MilenioscuroDerivative work:Janitoalevic · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Viceroyalty_of_Peru_(%2Bclaimed_Patagonian_and_Amazonic_territories)_(cropped).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Martín Hurtado de Arbieto founded the Spanish town of San Francisco de la Victoria de Vilcabamba, the old capital was abandoned to the forest, and the region was absorbed into the <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Upper Peru had been transferred from the <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b> to the new Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata in 1776, so both Lima and Buenos Aires claimed it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…former viceroy Santiago de Liniers was crushed and he was shot, but Paraguay and Montevideo swore to the Regency and the <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b> annexed Upper Peru, so the Junta sent armies against all three: the beginning of the Argentine War of Independence, before any independence was declared.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b> grew out of the conquest of the Inca Empire by Francisco Pizarro and his companions, who captured the Sapa Inca Atahualpa at Cajamarca in 1532 and entered Cuzco in 1533.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was composed between 1620 and 1631 by Juan Pérez de Bocanegra, a Franciscan priest who was also chief examiner of native languages for the Archdiocese of Cuzco, and published in 1631 in the <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hanacpachap-cussicuinin">Hanacpachap cussicuinin</a></li><li><blockquote>Lima became the capital of the <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b>, created in 1542, and the centre of Spanish rule in South America.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-18">18 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
